Example: Online Shopping

Suppose you're writing code for an online shopping application.

The main task is:

Login,
Select product,
Apply coupon (optional),
Payment,
Generate invoice

Here, applying the coupon is the specific part.

If applying the coupon fails, should the entire order fail? -> No.

The customer can still buy the product without the coupon.

So we give Apply Coupon its own try-catch.
-------------
Code :

try {                         // Outer try

    login();

    selectProduct();

    try {                     // Specific part
        applyCoupon();
    } catch (InvalidCouponException e) {
        System.out.println("Invalid coupon. Continuing without discount.");
    }

    payment();

    generateInvoice();

} catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("Order failed.");
}

---------------------
Explanation :

Why nested try?

Because only the "Apply Coupon" part needs separate exception handling.

If applyCoupon() fails:

✅ Handle it inside the inner catch.
✅ Continue with payment() and generateInvoice().

If payment() fails:

It is outside the inner try.
So the outer catch handles it, because payment is a critical part of placing the order.
