Another Example: Student Registration

Main task:

Fill personal details,
Upload profile photo (optional),
Submit registration
------------------
Code:

try {

    fillDetails();

    try {
        uploadPhoto();
    } catch (IOException e) {
        System.out.println("Photo upload failed.");
    }

    submitRegistration();

} catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("Registration failed.");
}
-----------------
Explanation :

Here, uploading the photo is the specific part.

If photo upload fails:

Registration should still continue.
So what does "specific part of the code" mean?

It means:

A small section inside a larger task that needs its own exception handling because its failure should not stop the entire operation.

Examples of a "specific part":

Applying a coupon during checkout.
Uploading a profile picture during registration.
Sending a notification email after an order is placed.
Writing a log file after processing data.

These are usually optional or independent operations, so we handle their exceptions separately.
